<section xml:id="sec-ch04-09-TripleIntegralsCylindricalSpherical" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Triple Integrals</title>
  <subtitle>in Cylindrical and Spherical Coordinates</subtitle>

  <p>
    We will now discuss the last two change of coordinates we will see in
    this class: cylindrical and spherical coordinates.
  </p>

  <note>
    <p>
      Any point in <m>x y z</m>-space can be expressed in the form
      ( <m>r, \theta, z</m> ) for <m>r \geq 0</m>,
      <m>0 \leq \theta \leq 2 \pi</m>, and <m>-\infty \lt z \lt \infty</m>
      as shown below:
    </p>
  </note>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-09-cylindrical-point">
    <caption>A point in cylindrical coordinates, rho, theta, z.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-01. -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-09-cylindrical-point" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-01.png">
          <shortdescription>A point in cylindrical coordinates, rho, theta, z.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>A point in cylindrical coordinates, <m>\rho</m>, <m>\theta</m>, <m>z</m>.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <note>
    <p>
      Cylindrical coordinates can simplify triple integrals in which the 3D
      region of integration is easily described in these coordinates. Below
      are a few examples of such regions:
    </p>
  </note>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-09-cylindrical-regions">
    <caption>From left to right: A cylindrical cell, a cone, a plane.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-01(1). -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-09-cylindrical-regions" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-01-1.png">
          <shortdescription>From left to right: A cylindrical cell, a cone, a plane.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>From left to right: A cylindrical cell, a cone, a plane.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <remark>
    <title>Formulas to Change from Cartesian to Cylindrical Coordinates</title>

    <p>
      Given a point ( <m>x, y, z</m> ) in 3-dimensional space, you can
      write it in cylindrical coordinates by using the formulas:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>r^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}</m></p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p><m>\tan (\theta)=\frac{y}{x}, x \neq 0</m></p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p><m>z=z</m></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Given a point ( <m>r, \theta, z</m> ) in 3-dimensional space, you can
      write it in Cartesian coordinates by using the formulas:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>x=r \cos (\theta)</m></p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p><m>y=r \sin (\theta)</m></p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p><m>z=z</m>.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <p>
    In practice, using cylindrical coordinates translates to evaluating the
    double integral of a triple integral using polar coordinates.
  </p>

  <remark>
    <title>Writing a Triple Integral in Cylindrical Coordinates</title>

    <p>To set up an iterated integral in cylindrical coordinates:</p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>Use the order <m>d z d r d \theta</m>.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Find the bounds with respect to <m>z</m> as we did in cartesian
            coordinates.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Project the 3 -dimensional region of integration <m>D</m> into
            the <m>x y</m> plane to get a 2 -dimensional region <m>R</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Set up the bounds of integration for <m>r</m> and <m>\theta</m>
            as if you were finding the area of <m>R</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The iterated integral should have the form
            <md>
              \int_{\alpha}^{\beta} \int_{g(\theta)}^{h(\theta)} \int_{G(r, \theta)}^{H(r, \theta)} f(r \cos (\theta), r \sin (\theta), z) r d z d r d \theta
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <title>Spherical Coordinates</title>

    <p>
      Any point in <m>x y z</m>-space can be expressed in the form
      ( <m>\rho, \varphi, \theta</m> ) for <m>\rho \geq 0</m>,
      <m>0 \leq \varphi \leq \pi</m>, and <m>0 \leq \theta \leq 2 \pi</m> as
      shown below:
    </p>
  </note>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-09-spherical-point">
    <caption>A point in spherical coordinates</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-07(3). -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-09-spherical-point" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-07-3.png">
          <shortdescription>A point in spherical coordinates</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>A point in spherical coordinates</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <remark>
    <p>
      The angle <m>\theta</m> is the same as in cylindrical coordinates
      (rotates 0 to <m>2 \pi</m> in the <m>x y</m>-plane). The variable
      <m>\rho</m> represents the radius of a sphere.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, the angle <m>\varphi</m> starts at the positive
      <m>z</m>-axis and rotates <m>\pi</m> radians ending at the negative
      <m>z</m>-axis.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      Spherical coordinates can simplify triple integrals in which the 3D
      region of integration is easily described in these coordinates. Below
      are a few examples of such regions:
    </p>
  </note>

  <!--Sphere radius a center ( $0,0, \mathrm{a}$ )\\
  $\{(\rho, \phi, \theta) \mid \rho=2 a \cos (\phi), 0 \leq \phi \leq \pi / 2\}, a>0$\\-->

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-09-spherical-regions">
    <caption>From left to right: A sphere, a cone, a plane.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to Screenshot 2025-11-04 154946-1.png. -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-09-spherical-regions" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/Screenshot-2025-11-04-154946-1.png">
          <shortdescription>From left to right: A sphere, a cone, a plane.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>From left to right: A sphere, a cone, a plane.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!--Cone\\
  $\left\{(\rho, \phi, \theta) \mid \phi=\phi_{0}\right\} \phi_{0} \neq 0, \pi / 2, \pi$\\
  \includegraphics[max width=0.3\textwidth, center]{2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-07}\\
  \includegraphics[max width=0.3\textwidth, right]{2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-07(1)}

  Shapes easily described in spherical coordinates-->

  <remark>
    <title>Formulas to Change from Cartesian to Spherical Coordinates</title>

    <p>
      Given a point ( <m>x, y, z</m> ) in 3-dimensional space, you can
      write it in spherical coordinates by using the formulas:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>\rho^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}</m></p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>Use trigonometry to find <m>\varphi</m> and <m>\theta</m>.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Given a point ( <m>\rho, \varphi, \theta</m> ) in 3-dimensional space,
      you can write it in Cartesian coordinates by using the formulas:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>x=\rho \sin (\varphi) \cos (\theta)</m></p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p><m>y=\rho \sin (\varphi) \sin (\theta)</m></p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p><m>z=\rho \cos (\varphi)</m>.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      Note that in spherical coordinates you need to replace <m>z</m> as
      well.
    </p>

    <p>
      You could geometrically (as we have done before) find the Jacobian of
      this change of variables. Instead, we will use the following formula.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Writing a Triple Integral in Spherical Coordinates</title>

    <p>To set up an iterated integral in cylindrical coordinates:</p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>Use the order <m>d\rho d \varphi d \theta</m>.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>Start from the origin <m>(0,0,0)</m> and radiate outwards</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The first function you encounter, preventing out from expanding
            outwards is the lower bound for <m>\rho</m>, the second is the
            upper bound.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Start at the positive <m>z</m>-axis and open outwards like a
            hand-held fan
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The first function you encounter, preventing out from fanning
            outwards is the lower bound for <m>\varphi</m>, the second is
            the upper bound.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            For <m>\theta</m>, similar with the polar coordinates, the
            bounds stem from the largest counter-clockwise rotation a point
            can do within the region of integration.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The iterated integral should have the form
            <md>
              \int_{\alpha}^{\beta} \int_{a}^{b} \int_{\rho=g(\varphi, \theta)}^{\rho=h(\varphi, \theta)} f(\rho, \varphi, \theta) \rho^{2} \sin (\varphi) d \rho d \varphi d \theta
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <title>Evaluating a Triple Integral for Volume</title>

    <p>
      As previously the integral that represents the volume of a region is
      calculated by the above integrals, by setting
      <m>f(\rho, \varphi, \theta) = 1 = f(r , \theta ,  z)</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-09-ex-22-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) a triple integral, in spherical
        coordinates, of <m>f(x,y,z)= x^2+y^2+z^2</m>  over the volume of
        the <q>ice cream cone</q> <m>D</m> bounded from above by the  sphere
        <m>\rho = 4 \cos\phi</m>, <m>\phi\leq \frac{\pi}{2}</m> and from
        below by the cone <m>\varphi=\frac{\pi}{6}</m> (above the
        <m>xy</m>-plane).
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-09-ex-22-1">
        <caption>The "ice cream cone" solid D, bounded by the sphere rho equals 4 cosine of phi, with phi less or equal to pi over 2, and the cone phi equals pi over 6.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-11. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-09-ex-22-1" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-11.png">
              <shortdescription>The "ice cream cone" solid D, bounded by the sphere rho equals 4 cosine of phi, with phi less or equal to pi over 2, and the cone phi equals pi over 6.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The <q>ice cream cone</q> solid <m>D</m>, bounded by the sphere <m>\rho=4\cos\varphi</m>, with <m>\varphi \leq \frac{\pi}{2}</m>, and the cone <m>\varphi=\frac{\pi}{6}</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Using <m>\rho^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}</m> the function
        <m>f(x,y,z) =  x^2+y^2+z^2</m> transforms to
        <m>f(\rho, \varphi , \theta )= \rho^2 </m>. The upper bound for
        <m>\rho</m> is given as <m>\rho = 4\cos\varphi \gt 0</m> while the
        lower bound is implied to be 0 since cones of the form
        <m>\varphi = c </m> pass through the origin (0,0,0), and when
        radiating away from the origin those cones do not obstruct.
      </p>

      <p>
        Since the sphere and the cone encompass a portion of the positive
        <m>z</m>-axis the lower bound for <m>\varphi</m> is  0, while the
        upper bound is provided as <m>\varphi = \frac{\pi}{6}</m>. Lastly
        there are no restrictions for <m>\theta</m> hence the bounds for
        integration are 0 and <m>2\pi</m> receptively.
      </p>

      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{6}}\int_0^{4\cos\varphi} f(\rho, \varphi , \theta) \rho^2  \sin \varphi\  d\rho d\varphi d\theta = \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{6}}\int_0^{4\cos\varphi} \rho^2 \rho^2   \sin \varphi\  d\rho d\varphi d\theta =</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{6}}\int_0^{4\cos\varphi}  \rho^4  \sin \varphi\  d\rho d\varphi d\theta</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-09-ex-22-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A solid region <m>D</m> lies inside the paraboloid
        <m>4-x^{2}-y^{2}-z=0</m>, and above the plane <m>z=3</m>, as shown
        below. Express the triple integral <m>\iiint_{D} 2z(x^2+y^2) d V</m>
        in cylindrical coordinates, then evaluate it.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-09-ex-22-2">
        <caption>The paraboloid 4 minus x squared minus y squared minus z equals 0, and the plane z equals 3.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-05. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-09-ex-22-2" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-05.png">
              <shortdescription>The paraboloid 4 minus x squared minus y squared minus z equals 0, and the plane z equals 3.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The paraboloid <m>4-x^2-y^2-z=0</m>, and the plane <m>z=3</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Using <m>r^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}</m> the function
        <m>f(x,y,z) =  2z(x^2+y^2)</m> transforms to
        <m>f(r, \theta , z )=2 zr^2 </m>. The paraboloid
        <m>z=4-x^2-y^2 = 4-(x^2+y^2)= 4-r^2</m> and the plane <m>z=3</m>
        intersect when
        <m>4-r^2 = 3 \Rightarrow r^2 = 1 \Rightarrow r=1</m>. Since for
        <m>r=0</m> the <m>z</m> coordinate of the paraboloid is
        <m>z=4-0=4 \gt 3</m>, the upper bound for z is given by the
        paraboloid and the lower by the plane.
      </p>

      <p>
        The finite region for <m>r</m> of the above intersection is given
        by <m>0\leq r \leq 1</m>. Lastly there are no restrictions for
        <m>\theta</m> hence the bounds for integration are 0 and
        <m>2\pi</m> receptively.
      </p>

      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{1}\int_3^{4-r^2} (2zr^2)r\  dz dr d\theta = \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{1}\int_3^{4-r^2} 2zr^3 \ dz dr d\theta = \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{1} \left . r^3z^2 \right |_3^{4-r^2} dr d\theta =</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{1} r^3(4-r^2)^2- 9r^2\ dr d\theta =  \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{1} 16r^3-8r^5+r^7 - 9r ^3 \ dr d\theta  =</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^{2\pi}\left . 4r^4-\frac{4}{3}r^6+\frac{1}{8} r^8 - \frac{9}{4} r ^4 \right |_0^1 d\theta = \int_0^{2\pi} 4-\frac{4}{3}+\frac{1}{8}  - \frac{9}{4} d\theta  = \int_0^{2pi} \frac{13}{24} d\theta = \left . \frac{13}{24}\theta \right |_0^{2\pi} =\frac{13}{12}\pi</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!--\setcounter{tcb@cnt@example}{1}
  \begin{example}[Continuation of 22.2]
  A solid region $D$ lies inside the paraboloid $4-x^{2}-y^{2}-z=0$, and above the plane $z=3$, as shown below. Express the triple integral $\iiint_{D} 2z(x^2+y^2) d V$ in cylindrical coordinates, then evaluate it.\\
   \vspace{0.5cm}\end{example}-->

  <definition>
    <title>Jacobian in Three Dimensions and Integrals</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>T: x=g(u, v, w), y=h(u, v, w)</m>, and <m>z=q(u, v, w)</m> be
        a transformation that maps a closed and bounded region <m>S</m> in
        <m>u v w</m>-space to a region <m>D</m> in <m>x y z</m>-space.
        Assume <m>T</m> is one-to-one on the interior of <m>S</m> and that
        <m>g, h</m>, and <m>q</m> have continuous first partial derivatives
        there. If <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>D</m>, then
        <md>
          \iiint_{D} f(x, y, z) d V=\iiint_{S} f(g(u, v, w), h(u, v, w), q(u, v, w))|J(u, v, w)| d V
        </md>
        where
        <md>
          <mrow>J(u, v, w) \amp =\frac{\partial(x, y, z)}{\partial(u, v, w)}=\left|\begin{array}{lll} x_{u} \amp x_{v} \amp x_{w} \\ y_{u} \amp y_{v} \amp y_{w} \\ z_{u} \amp z_{v} \amp z_{w} \end{array}\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp =x_{u}\left(y_{v} z_{w}-y_{w} z_{v}\right)-x_{v}\left(y_{u} z_{w}-y_{w} z_{u}\right)+x_{w}\left(y_{u} z_{v}-y_{v} z_{u}\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp =z_{u}\left(x_{v} y_{w}-x_{w} y_{v}\right)-z_{v}\left(x_{u} y_{w}-x_{w} y_{u}\right)+z_{w}\left(x_{u} y_{v}-x_{v} y_{u}\right)</mrow>
        </md>
        is the Jacobian of the transformation <m>T</m> which measures how
        volume around a point ( <m>x, y, z</m> ) change as we move to
        <m>u v w</m>-space.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-09-ex-22-3">
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>u=\rho, v=\varphi</m>, and <m>w=\theta</m>. Then,</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>J(\rho, \varphi, \theta)  =\left|\begin{array}{ccc} \sin (\varphi) \cos (\theta) \amp \rho \cos (\varphi) \cos (\theta) \amp -\rho \sin (\varphi) \sin (\theta)  \\ \sin (\varphi) \sin (\theta) \amp \rho\cos (\varphi) \sin (\theta) \amp \rho \sin (\varphi) \cos (\theta)  \\ \cos (\varphi) \amp -\rho \sin (\varphi) \amp 0 \end{array}\right| =</mrow>
          <mrow>\sin\varphi\cos\theta \left | \begin{matrix} \rho\cos\varphi\sin\theta \amp \rho\sin\varphi\cos\theta  \\ -\rho\sin\varphi \amp 0 \end{matrix} \right | - \rho\cos\varphi\cos\theta  \left | \begin{matrix} \sin\varphi\sin\theta \amp \rho\sin\varphi\cos\theta\\ \cos\varphi \amp 0 \end{matrix} \right |</mrow>
          <mrow>+(-\rho\sin\varphi\sin\theta) \left | \begin{matrix} \sin\varphi\sin\theta \amp \rho\cos\varphi\sin\theta  \\ \cos\varphi \amp -\rho\sin\varphi \end{matrix} \right | =</mrow>
          <mrow>\rho^2\sin^3\varphi\cos^2\theta +\rho^2\cos^2\varphi\sin^2\theta\sin\varphi +(-\rho\sin\varphi\sin\theta)\left ( -\rho\sin^2\varphi\sin\theta -\rho\cos^2\varphi\sin\theta \right)  =</mrow>
          <mrow>\rho^2\sin^3\varphi\cos^2\theta +\rho^2\cos^2\varphi\sin^2\theta\sin\varphi +\rho^2\sin^3\varphi\sin^2\theta+ \rho^2\cos^2\varphi\sin\varphi\cos^2\theta  =</mrow>
          <mrow>\rho^2\sin^3\varphi (\cos^2\theta +\sin^2\theta)+\rho^2\sin\varphi\cos^2</mrow>
          <mrow>varphi (\sin^2 \theta + \cos^2\theta)=</mrow>
          <mrow>\rho^2\sin\varphi (\sin^2\varphi +\cos^2\varphi)= \rho^2\sin\varphi</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Since <m>0 \leq \varphi \leq \pi, \sin (\varphi) \geq 0</m>. It
        follows that
        <md>
          \mid J\left(\rho, \varphi, \theta\left)|=\left|\rho^{2} \sin (\varphi)\right|=\rho^{2} \sin (\varphi) \right.\right.
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-09-ex-22-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) a triple integral that gives the
        volume of the <q>ice cream cone</q> <m>D</m> below the  sphere
        <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=4</m>, and above the cone
        <m>z=\sqrt 3 \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Using <m>\rho^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}</m> the sphere
        <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=4</m> transforms to
        <m>\rho^2 =4 \Rightarrow \rho  =2</m> (Restricting the values of
        <m>r</m> to be non-negative). Using <m>z=\rho \cos\varphi</m>,
        <m>x=\rho\sin\varphi\cos\theta</m> and
        <m>y=\rho\sin\varphi\sin\theta</m> the cone transforms to
        <m>\rho \cos\varphi = \sqrt 3 \sqrt{ \rho^2\sin^2\varphi\cos^2\theta + \rho^2\sin^2\varphi\sin^2\theta  } =  \sqrt 3\sqrt{ \rho^2\sin^2\varphi(\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta ) } = \sqrt 3\sqrt{ \rho^2\sin^2\varphi } =\sqrt 3\rho\sin\varphi </m>
        since <m>\sin\varphi \geq 0 </m> for <m>0\leq \varphi \leq \pi</m>.
        This implies that for <m>\rho\not = 0</m>,
        <m>\tan \varphi = \frac{\sqrt 3}{3}\Rightarrow \varphi= \frac{\pi}{6}</m>
        (The value <m>\varphi = \frac{7\pi}{6}</m> is disregarded since we
        need <m>0\leq \varphi \leq \pi</m>).
      </p>

      <p>
        The upper bound for <m>\rho</m> is given as <m>\rho = 4</m> while
        the lower bound is implied to be 0 since cones of the form
        <m>\varphi = c </m> pass through the origin (0,0,0), and when
        radiating away from the origin those cones do not obstruct.
      </p>

      <p>
        Since the sphere and the cone encompass a portion of the positive
        <m>z</m>-axis (between <m>z=0</m> and <m>z=2</m>) the lower bound
        for <m>\varphi</m> is  0, while the upper bound is provided as
        <m>\varphi = \frac{\pi}{6}</m>. Lastly there are no restrictions for
        <m>\theta</m> hence the bounds for integration are 0 and
        <m>2\pi</m> receptively.
      </p>

      <p>
        <md>
          \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{6}}\int_0^{2} \rho^2  \sin \varphi\  d\rho d\varphi d\theta
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-09-ex-22-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) a triple integral in spherical
        coordinates that gives the volume inside the ball <m>\rho \leq 1</m>
        and above the plane <m>z=1/2</m> (pictured below).
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-09-ex-22-5">
        <caption>The ball of radius 1 centred at the origin, and two planes z equals 0 and z equals one half.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-14. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-09-ex-22-5" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-14.png">
              <shortdescription>The ball of radius 1 centred at the origin, and two planes z equals 0 and z equals one half.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The ball of radius 1 centred at the origin, and two planes <m>z=0</m> and <m>z=\frac{1}{2}</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Using <m>\rho^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}</m> the sphere
        <m>x^2+y^2+z^2\leq 1</m> transforms to <m>\rho\leq 1 </m>
        (Restricting the values of <m>r</m> to be non-negative). Using
        <m>z=\rho \cos\varphi</m>, the plane transforms to
        <m>\rho \cos\varphi = \frac{1}{2}</m>. To find the intersection of
        the two surfaces:
        <md>
          \left . \begin{matrix} \rho \cos \varphi \amp = \amp \frac{1}{2} \\ \rho \amp = \amp 1 \end{matrix} \right \} \Rightarrow \cos\varphi = \frac{1}{2} \Rightarrow \varphi = \frac{\pi}{3}
        </md>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Since the sphere and the cone encompass a portion of the positive
        <m>z</m>-axis (between <m>z=1/2</m> and <m>z=1</m>)the lower bound
        for <m>\varphi</m> is  0, while the upper bound is provided as
        <m>\varphi = \frac{\pi}{3}</m>. As far as <m>\rho</m> is concerned
        the upper bound is 1, the lower bound is implied by the equation
        <m>\rho\cos\varphi = \frac{1}{2}</m> to be
        <m>\rho = \frac{1}{2}\sec \varphi</m>. Lastly there are no
        restrictions for <m>\theta</m> hence the bounds for integration are
        0 and <m>2\pi</m> receptively.
      </p>

      <p>
        <md>
          \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{3}}\int_{\frac{1}{2}\sec\varphi}^{1} \rho^2   \sin \varphi\ d\rho d\varphi d\theta
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!--\setcounter{tcb@cnt@example}{4}
  \begin{example}[Continuation of 22.1]
  A solid region $D$ lies inside the paraboloid $4-x^{2}-y^{2}-z=0$, and above the plane $z=3$, as shown below. Express the triple integral $\iiint_{D} zd V$ in cylindrical coordinates, then evaluate it.\\
   \vspace{0.5cm}\end{example}-->

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-09-ex-22-6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the volume of the sphere <m>x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=a^{2}, a \gt 0</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-09-ex-22-6">
        <caption>A sphere of radius a, where a is greater than 0, centred at the origin.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-10. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-09-ex-22-6" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-10.png">
              <shortdescription>A sphere of radius a, where a is greater than 0, centred at the origin.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>A sphere of radius <m>a</m>, where <m>a \gt 0</m>, centred at the origin.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Using <m>\rho^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}</m> the sphere
        <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=a^2</m> transforms to
        <m>\rho^2 =a^2 \Rightarrow \rho  =a</m> (Restricting the values of
        <m>r</m> to be non-negative). There are no restriction on
        <m>\varphi</m> and <m>\theta</m>, hence their respective range is
        implied to be <m>0\leq \varphi \pi</m> and
        <m>0\leq \theta\leq 2\pi</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\pi}\int_{0}^{a} \rho^2 \sin \varphi \  d\rho d\varphi d\theta =  \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\pi}\left. \frac{\rho^3}{3} \sin \varphi \right |_{0}^{a} d\varphi d\theta= \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\pi} \frac{a^3}{3} \sin \varphi\ d\varphi d\theta =</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^{2\pi}\left . -\frac{a^3}{3} \cos \varphi \right |_0^{\pi}  d\theta =  \int_0^{2\pi}  -\frac{a^3}{3} \cos \pi +\frac{a^3}{3} \cos 0\  d\theta =  \int_0^{2\pi}  \frac{2a^3}{3}  d\theta = \left .   \frac{2a^3}{3} \theta \right |_0^{2\pi} = \frac{4\pi a^3}{3}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-09-ex-22-7">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) a triple integral in spherical
        coordinates that gives the volume inside the ball <m>\rho \leq 2</m>
        and but outside the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=1</m> (from OpenStax).
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-09-ex-22-7">
        <caption>The ball rho less or equal to 2 and the cylinder x squared plus y squared equals 1.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-12. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-09-ex-22-7" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-12.png">
              <shortdescription>The ball rho less or equal to 2 and the cylinder x squared plus y squared equals 1.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The ball <m>\rho \leq 2</m> and the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Using <m>x=\rho\sin\varphi\cos\theta</m> and
        <m>y=\rho\sin\varphi\sin\theta</m>, the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>
        transforms to
        <m>\rho^2 \sin^2\varphi\cos^2\theta + \rho^2 \sin^2\varphi\sin^2\theta = 1 \Rightarrow \rho^2 \sin^2\varphi (\cos^2\theta +\sin^2\theta )=1 \Rightarrow \rho^2 \sin^2\varphi = 1 \Rightarrow</m>
        <m>\rho \sin\varphi = 1</m>, since we restrict <m>\rho</m> to have
        non-negative values and <m>\varphi</m> to be between <m>0</m> and
        <m>\pi</m>. To find the intersection of the two surfaces:
        <md>
          \left . \begin{matrix} \rho \sin\varphi \amp = \amp 1 \\ \rho \amp = \amp 2 \end{matrix} \right \} \Rightarrow \sin\varphi = \frac{1}{2} \Rightarrow \varphi = \frac{\pi}{6} \text{ or } \frac{5\pi}{6}
        </md>.
        To  investigate whether or not one needs to take into account the
        values of <m>\varphi</m> from <m>0</m> to <m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m>, and
        from <m>\frac{5\pi}{6}</m> to <m>\pi</m>, notice that the
        <m>z</m>-axis (<m>\varphi = 0</m> or <m>\varphi = \pi</m>) lie
        inside NOT outside the cylinder since any point of the form
        <m>P(0,0,a)</m> has <m>x_P^2+y_P^2 = 0+0 = 0 \lt 1</m> . Hence, the
        bounds for <m>\varphi</m> are <m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m> and
        <m>\frac{5\pi}{6}</m>. As far as <m>\rho</m> is concerned the upper
        bound is 2, the lower bound is implied by the equation
        <m>\rho\sin\varphi = 1</m> to be <m>\csc \varphi</m>. Lastly there
        are no restrictions for <m>\theta</m> hence the bounds for
        integration are 0 and <m>2\pi</m> receptively.
        <md>
          \int_0^{2\pi}\int_{\frac{\pi}{6}}^{\frac{5\pi}{6}}\int_{\csc\varphi}^{2} \rho^2   \sin \varphi\ d\rho d\varphi d\theta
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-09-ex-22-8">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) a triple integral using cylindrical
        coordinates that gives the 4D net volume bounded by a function
        <m>f(r, \theta, z)</m> and <m>x y z</m>-space, on a region <m>D</m>
        bounded below by the plane <m>z=0</m>, laterally by the circular
        cylinder <m>(x-1)^{2}+y^{2}=1</m>, and above by the paraboloid
        <m>z=4-x^{2}-y^{2}</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-09-ex-22-8">
        <caption>The volume bounded below by the plane z equals 0, laterally by the circular cylinder, x minus 1, squared, plus y squared equals 1, and above by the paraboloid z equals 4 minus x squared minus y squared.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-03. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-09-ex-22-8" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_11_04_0bc814412e6d5de1fb6fg-03.png">
              <shortdescription>The volume bounded below by the plane z equals 0, laterally by the circular cylinder, x minus 1, squared, plus y squared equals 1, and above by the paraboloid z equals 4 minus x squared minus y squared.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The volume bounded below by the plane <m>z=0</m>, laterally by the circular cylinder <m>(x-1)^2+y^2=1</m>, and above by the paraboloid <m>z=4-x^2-y^2</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Using <m>r^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}</m> the paraboloid
        <m>z= 4-(x^2+y^2)</m> transforms to <m>z=4-r^2 </m>. For the
        cylinder <m>(x-1)^{2}+y^{2}=1</m>, notice that it lies above the
        first and forth quadrants. hence <m>\theta</m> much be on a
        sub-interval of <m>(-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{2})</m>. Utilizing
        <m>x=r\cos\theta</m> and <m>y=r\sin\theta</m>, one has
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>(x-1)^2+y^2= 1 \Rightarrow (r\cos\theta - )^2+r^2\sin^2\theta = 1 \Rightarrow r^2\cos^2\theta -2r\cos\theta +1 +r^2\sin^2\theta = 1 \Rightarrow</mrow>
          <mrow>r^2(\cos^2\theta +\sin^2\theta )-2r\cos\theta = 0 \Rightarrow r^2-2r\cos\theta = 0 \Rightarrow r(r-2\cos\theta) = 0\Rightarrow r= 0 \text{ or } r= 2\cos\theta</mrow>
        </md>
        For the range of <m>\theta</m> mentioned above,
        <m>r=2\cos\theta \geq 0</m> and hence one has the lower and upper
        bound for <m>r</m> as <m>0</m> and <m>2\cos\theta</m> respectively.
        Lastly, to verify that the <m>\theta</m> ranges over
        <m>(-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{2})</m> and not a subinterval, observe
        that the second coordinate of the points of this circle <m>y_c</m>
        satisfies <m>y_c = r \sin\theta = 2\cos\theta\sin\theta</m>. In this
        expression for <m>\theta = -\frac{\pi}{2}</m> the coordinate is 0,
        in between <m>-\frac{\pi}{2}</m> and <m>0</m> is negative, between
        <m>0</m> and <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m> is positive and at
        <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{2}</m> the coordinate is 0 again. Hence we
        traverse the circle exactly once over this interval of
        <m>\theta</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        (A more rigorous argument will involve to suppose that there are 2
        value of <m>\theta</m> in this interval, say <m>\theta_1</m> and
        <m>\theta_2</m>, for which the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are fixed and
        arrive to a contradiction, but this previous argument is adequate
        for the purpose of the course in the case that we cannot draw a
        graph).
        <md>
          \int_{-\frac{\pi}{2}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}}\int_0^{2\cos\theta}\int_0^{4-r^2} r\  dz dr d\theta
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!--\setcounter{tcb@cnt@example}{7}
  \begin{example}[Continuation of 22.1]
   Set up (but do not evaluate) a triple integral using cylindrical coordinates that gives the 4D net volume bounded by a function $f(r, \theta, z)$ and $x y z$-space, on a region $D$ bounded below by the plane $z=0$, laterally by the circular cylinder $(x-1)^{2}+y^{2}=1$, and above by the paraboloid $z=4-x^{2}-y^{2}$.\vspace{0.5cm}\end{example}-->

</section>
